The accelerated progress of quantum computing presents an emerging cybersecurity threat to widely deployed public-key cryptographic systems. Algorithms such as RSA and Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC), which currently protect digital communications, authentication, digital signatures, certificates, and sensitive enterprise data, could become vulnerable to sufficiently capable quantum computers. This risk, including “harvest now, decrypt later” attacks against long-lived sensitive information, highlights the need for organizations to prepare their cryptographic environments for the post-quantum era.

To address this challenge, the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) has standardized Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) algorithms designed to resist attacks from both classical and quantum computers. These include Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM) for key establishment and Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Algorithm (ML-DSA) for digital signatures.

This paper proposes a practical enterprise PQC migration framework focusing on cryptographic asset discovery, identification of quantum-vulnerable algorithms, risk classification, crypto-agility, and phased adoption of NIST-standardized PQC algorithms. The framework helps identify cryptographic dependencies across applications, databases, certificates, APIs, and communication protocols and prioritizes their migration based on security and business requirements.

A Proof of Concept (POC) validates the proposed framework by implementing conventional and post-quantum cryptographic mechanisms in a controlled environment. The POC compares RSA/ECC and PQC approaches based on cryptographic operation time, key, ciphertext and signature sizes, computational overhead, compatibility, and implementation complexity. Automation is incorporated to demonstrate cryptographic discovery and PQC readiness assessment.

The proposed framework provides a structured approach for organizations to assess their current cryptographic exposure, evaluate NIST-standardized PQC technologies, and develop a phased transition toward quantum-safe cybersecurity while maintaining compatibility and minimizing disruption to existing enterprise infrastructure.
